item.text:
Discovery wave 1 is complete. I kept this strictly as research, not scoring.

The chapter picture is mixed but not especially favorable: China’s official 2022 line is NFU, minimum deterrence, and arms-control language, but independent technical and policy sources show a major nuclear buildup, more silo construction, expanding strategic deterrence ambitions, and weaker crisis communication after the Pelosi/Taiwan episode. That is enough to map the chapter, but not enough to score it yet.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E001","title":"(CPC Congress) CPC expounds on main objectives, tasks for next 5 years","publisher":"Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-10-16","url":"https://english.news.cn/20221016/8ecf5c8af8be476796ca1fbf556754a4/c.html","claim":"The 20th Party Congress agenda prioritized further consolidation of national security and the PLA centenary goals for 2027.","locator":"lines 16-29","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Datelined Xinhua congress report summarizing the party's published objectives.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period policy framing.","ruler_attribution":"Party-state priorities under Xi Jinping's 2022 leadership; useful as inherited/posture context rather than personal nuclear command proof.","contrary_evidence":["This is broad security framing, not nuclear command or restraint evidence.","Independent technical sources show large-scale nuclear modernization continued."],"lenses":["1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E002","title":"China and the United States: It's a Cold War, but don't panic","publisher":"Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists","publication_date":"2022-03-10","url":"https://thebulletin.org/premium/2022-03/china-and-the-united-states-its-a-cold-war-but-dont-panic/","claim":"The article links Xi Jinping to a rapid military buildup, including nuclear expansion, and argues he wants the armed forces to be commensurate with China's international standing.","locator":"lines 144-154","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Analytical secondary source that explicitly connects Xi's leadership goals to military and nuclear buildup.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporary 2022 interpretation of Xi-era posture.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributes the buildup direction to Xi as CCP general secretary.","contrary_evidence":["It is an interpretive essay, not a primary document.","The piece does not prove any specific nuclear-use decision."],"lenses":["1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E003","title":"The Chinese Delegation Attends the 10th Review Conference of the Parties to the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://www.fmprc.gov.cn/eng/wjb/zzjg_663340/jks_665232/jkxw_665234/202208/t20220805_10735079.html","claim":"China told the NPT review conference that its nuclear strategy is self-defense, that it maintains an NFU posture, keeps capability at the minimum needed for national security, and treats that posture as a contribution to risk reduction.","locator":"lines 70-73","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official 2022 delegation statement with direct policy language and clear date fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period statement at the NPT review conference.","ruler_attribution":"Official PRC policy under Xi's 2022 leadership, but still institutional rather than personal operational proof.","contrary_evidence":["Independent monitors report simultaneous strategic expansion and silo construction.","Official self-description does not establish implementation or restraint quality."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.3","1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E004","title":"Statement by H.E. Amb. LI Song on Nuclear Disarmament at the Tenth NPT Review Conference","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://www.mfa.gov.cn/eng/wjb/zzjg_663340/jks_665232/kjfywj_665252/202406/t20240606_11405407.html","claim":"China said it supports early CTBT entry into force, keeps to a nuclear test moratorium, backs FMCT negotiations, and supports P5 strategic-risk-reduction work.","locator":"lines 89-93","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dated NPT conference statement with concrete arms-control positions and implementation claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 review-conference evidence, even though archived on a later MFA page.","ruler_attribution":"PRC delegation position under Xi-era policy; not personal operational attribution.","contrary_evidence":["China still had not ratified the CTBT.","The same year saw major modernization and silo expansion."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E005","title":"Statement by H.E. Amb. LI Song on Nuclear Non-Proliferation at the Tenth NPT Review Conference","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-08-08","url":"https://www.mfa.gov.cn/eng/wjb/zzjg_663340/jks_665232/kjfywj_665252/202406/t20240606_11405408.html","claim":"China argued that AUKUS nuclear-submarine cooperation and nuclear sharing are proliferation risks and urged political and diplomatic handling of the Korean Peninsula and Iran to avoid escalation.","locator":"lines 73-95","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary delegation statement with specific, dated positions on nonproliferation and escalation management.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 NPT review-conference evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Official Chinese policy stance under Xi's leadership.","contrary_evidence":["This is a position paper, not proof of compliance by itself.","The statement is hostile to rival programs, but does not show Chinese restraint in its own buildup."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.6","1B.8","1B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E006","title":"JOINT STATEMENT Of the Leaders of the Five Nuclear-Weapon States On Preventing Nuclear War and Avoiding Arms Races","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the People's Republic of China","publication_date":"2022-01-03","url":"https://www.mfa.gov.cn/eng/wjb/zzjg_663340/xos_664404/xwlb_664406/202201/t20220103_10478505.html","claim":"The five nuclear-weapon-state leaders jointly said they should avoid war, keep nuclear weapons defensive, prevent spread, preserve nonproliferation and arms-control commitments, and strengthen measures against unauthorized or unintended use.","locator":"lines 64-70","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct leader-level joint statement dated to the target year with explicit risk-reduction language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period statement.","ruler_attribution":"China as one signatory among the five NWS; useful as leader-level diplomatic context, not China-specific proof of restraint.","contrary_evidence":["Later in 2022, China cut some military communication channels after Pelosi's Taiwan visit.","General statement language does not prove implementation."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.3","1B.4","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E007","title":"2022 Report on Military and Security Developments Involving the People's Republic of China","publisher":"U.S. Department of Defense","publication_date":"2022-11-29","url":"https://www.defense.gov/News/Releases/Release/Article/3230516/2022-report-on-military-and-security-developments-involving-the-peoples-republi/","claim":"The Pentagon said China was strengthening strategic deterrence across nuclear, space, cyber, electronic-warfare, and counterspace domains, had more than 400 operational nuclear warheads, and could reach about 1,500 warheads by 2035 if the trend continued.","locator":"lines 255-265","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative contemporaneous U.S. defense assessment with specific warhead and capability estimates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 assessment of the target-year posture.","ruler_attribution":"Assesses Xi-led PRC modernization, but is still an external estimate rather than internal Chinese admission.","contrary_evidence":["China rejects the Pentagon's projections.","The report itself describes capability growth, not actual nuclear use or crisis behavior."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E008","title":"10. World nuclear forces","publisher":"Stockholm International Peace Research Institute","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.sipri.org/yearbook/2022/10","claim":"SIPRI assessed China as being in a significant nuclear modernization and expansion phase, with over 300 new missile silos and very limited transparency about force numbers and plans.","locator":"lines 88-101","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Leading independent technical yearbook with open-source force assessment and direct China chapter summary.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 yearbook assessment of the target-year posture.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level posture under Xi-era leadership; inherited and ongoing strategic context.","contrary_evidence":["China says it keeps forces at the minimum level required.","SIPRI's note is about force posture, not command discipline or use intent."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E009","title":"China Is Building A Second Nuclear Missile Silo Field","publisher":"Federation of American Scientists","publication_date":"2021-07-26","url":"https://fas.org/publication/china-is-building-a-second-nuclear-missile-silo-field/","claim":"FAS said the Yumen and Hami silo buildout was the most significant expansion of the Chinese nuclear arsenal ever and that roughly 250 silos were under construction, which it argued moved China beyond a minimum-deterrence posture.","locator":"lines 63-66 and 91-100","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Open-source satellite analysis by a leading nuclear-information NGO with concrete imagery-based conclusions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 inherited baseline that directly conditions the 2022 posture.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited strategic posture under Xi's era rather than a one-year decision point.","contrary_evidence":["China's official doctrine still describes the force as minimum and defensive.","The article does not prove warhead loading or readiness."],"lenses":["1B.10","1B.3"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E010","title":"STRATCOM Says China Has More ICBM Launchers Than The United States - We Have Questions","publisher":"Federation of American Scientists","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://fas.org/publication/stratcom-says-china-has-more-icbm-launchers-than-the-united-states/","claim":"FAS argued that STRATCOM's October 2022 count implied more than 450 Chinese ICBM launchers, but that silo loading, support facilities, command-and-control systems, and security perimeters were still incomplete or uncertain.","locator":"lines 52-70","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Open-source technical analysis directly tied to the October 2022 cutoff and specific operational uncertainty.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2022 force posture.","ruler_attribution":"Xi-era force posture and operational readiness, not personal command-order attribution.","contrary_evidence":["This is an inference from satellite imagery and Pentagon reporting, not a Chinese admission.","Operational uncertainty cuts both ways: more capability does not prove immediate use risk."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E011","title":"Engaging China on Strategic Stability and Mutual Vulnerability","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2022/10/engaging-china-on-strategic-stability-and-mutual-vulnerability?lang=en","claim":"Perkovich argued that China was building hundreds of silos, that U.S.-China relations were exposed to nuclear mass destruction, and that stability dialogue was needed because conventional, cyber, and nuclear systems are entangled.","locator":"lines 65-85 and 106-113","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"High-quality policy scholarship offering an explicit strategic-stability framing rather than a raw data claim.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period strategic context.","ruler_attribution":"Contextualizes Xi-era decision-making and security competition.","contrary_evidence":["This is normative policy analysis, not proof of a specific Xi decision.","It emphasizes mutual vulnerability and dialogue more than culpability."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E012","title":"China halts high-level military dialogue with U.S., suspends other cooperation","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-05","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/beijing-halts-highlevel-military-dialogue-with-us-suspends-other-cooperation-2864888","claim":"After Pelosi's Taiwan visit, China suspended dialogue between senior military commanders and canceled a planned maritime military security-mechanism meeting, worsening crisis-management channels.","locator":"lines 418-424","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on a concrete policy decision with direct escalation implications.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis-management evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Chinese state decision under Xi's leadership; attribution is to the PRC leadership, not to a nuclear-specific order.","contrary_evidence":["Beijing framed the suspension as retaliation for Pelosi's visit.","The article does not itself show nuclear-use intent."],"lenses":["1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E013","title":"Ending Nuclear Tests","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"undated (accessed 2026-07-22)","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/our-mission/ending-nuclear-tests","claim":"CTBTO notes that China is still among the Annex 2 states whose ratification is required for the CTBT to enter into force, even though the treaty has broad support and a strong anti-test norm.","locator":"lines 245-255","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IGO treaty-status page with explicit ratification status and treaty-function explanation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable treaty-status context applicable in 2022 and thereafter.","ruler_attribution":"State-level treaty posture under Xi-era policy.","contrary_evidence":["China separately says it supports CTBT entry into force and maintains a moratorium on testing.","Non-ratification does not prove imminent testing."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"evidence_id":"E014","title":"Arms Control and Proliferation Profile: China","publisher":"Arms Control Association","publication_date":"2023-06","url":"https://www.armscontrol.org/factsheets/arms-control-and-proliferation-profile-china","claim":"The profile says China still publicly maintains NFU, but its modernization is moving toward a launch-on-warning posture and the expansion challenges the claim that the arsenal remains at a minimal level.","locator":"lines 255-256 and 118-122","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-established arms-control NGO synthesis that directly contrasts declaratory policy with modernization trends.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but directly relevant to the 2022 posture trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"Xi-era nuclear posture and operational trajectory, not a one-year operational order.","contrary_evidence":["China continues to repeat NFU and defensive doctrine in official statements.","The article describes expected capabilities, not proof that launch-on-warning was already operational in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.3","1B.10"]}

Search ledger:
- Candidates found: about 55 plausible documents across official Chinese, treaty, technical, scholarly, and media families.
- Documents opened: 31 promising documents, with follow-on line-level inspection on the strongest ones.
- Duplicate/syndicated families discarded: about 18, mostly Reuters mirrors, repeated official reposts, and repeated CTBTO/FA analyses.
- Irrelevant or out-of-period items discarded: about 9.
- Inaccessible or internal-error items discarded: about 4.
- Accepted distinct URLs: 14.
- Distinct domains retained: 10.

Material gaps and next best directions:
- I still want a cleaner primary source on Xi’s direct office/authority chain over nuclear forces in 2022, ideally the official 20th Party Congress first-plenary communique or the leadership list in an accessible English or Chinese source with line-level text.
- I want a stronger command-and-control source specific to the PLA Rocket Force, early-warning, custody, or launch posture, not just general strategic-deterrence discussion.
- I want one or two better PRC primary sources on dual-use catastrophe-risk policy in 2022, especially cyber, space, AI, or biological governance tied to Xi or the central state.
- I would next search for Chinese-language MOD/CMC pages on Rocket Force modernization and for treaty/verification records on CTBT/NPT with direct Chinese participation text.
